<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=wheelbarrow">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wheelbarrow</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">118</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A wheelbarrow is a small hand-propelled vehicle, usually with one wheel, pushed and guided by a single person using two handles at the rear. The earliest evidence comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>: a painted tomb mural at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, dated to 118 CE, shows a man pushing one. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms">Records of the Three Kingdoms</a> credit the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang">Zhuge Liang</a> with a 'wooden ox' that carried military supplies in 231 CE. Chinese wheelbarrows mostly carried the load over a large central wheel, while those that appeared in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> between 1170 and 1250 had the wheel at or near the front.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wheelbarrow.jpg" alt="Wheelbarrow" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ildar Sagdejev (Specious) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2008-07-15_Construction_wheelbarrow_at_Duke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>118 CE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Chengdu, Sichuan, China</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The Records of the Three Kingdoms credit Zhuge Liang with its invention in 231 CE; M. J. T. Lewis read a 'one-wheeler' in temple inventories at Eleusis of 408–406 BCE as a Greek wheelbarrow, but there is no evidence for it in Greece or Rome.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/wheel">Wheel<span>before 4000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=wheelbarrow">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
